    faq=[
        ("What is the difference between an epic, a story and a task?", "An epic is a large goal that takes several Sprints, a user story is a small slice of that goal that delivers value to a user within one Sprint, and a task is a technical step, usually a day or less, needed to finish a story."),
        ("Is a task smaller than a story?", "In the generic Agile hierarchy, yes: tasks are steps inside a story. In Jira, a Task is a peer of a Story on the same level and is not necessarily smaller; the step inside a story is called a Sub-task."),
        ("Do tasks need story points?", "Usually not. Story points are applied to stories so the team can forecast. Tasks are often estimated in hours or simply counted. Do not add task hours to story points."),
        ("What is the difference between a task and a sub-task in Jira?", "A Jira Task sits on the same level as a Story and Bug and represents work without a user-facing outcome. A Sub-task belongs to a parent item (a Story, Task or Bug) and represents one step of it."),
        ("What comes above an epic?", "Many organisations group epics under themes or initiatives that link to strategic objectives or OKRs. In Jira, Atlassian says levels above epic are available on the Premium plan."),
        ("What is the difference between an epic and a feature?", "Where both are used, as in SAFe, a feature is a capability that sits between an epic and stories and can usually be delivered within a planning increment. Many single teams skip features and use only epics and stories."),
        ("How many user stories should an epic have?", "There is no rule. Many epics contain somewhere between a handful and a few dozen stories. If an epic keeps growing and never closes, split it into smaller epics with their own goals."),
        ("Who writes user stories and tasks?", "The Product Owner is accountable for the Product Backlog, but stories are usually written with the Developers and stakeholders. Tasks are created by the Developers, mainly in Sprint Planning."),
        ("Can a story exist without an epic?", "Yes. Small, standalone stories such as a single improvement or bug fix do not need a parent epic."),
        ("Where do bugs fit in the hierarchy?", "Bugs found after release are usually ordered in the Product Backlog at story level, sometimes linked to an epic. Defects found while building a story are fixed as tasks within that story."),
        ("Are epics and user stories part of Scrum?", "No. The 2020 Scrum Guide describes Product Backlog items and a Sprint Backlog but does not prescribe epics, stories or tasks. They are complementary practices many Scrum Teams use."),
        ("Can non-software teams use epics, stories and tasks?", "Yes. A marketing team might have an epic for a product launch, stories such as 'as a prospect, I want a comparison page so that I can choose a plan', and tasks for copy, design and review."),
        ("Where should I track epics, stories and tasks?", "In any backlog or work management tool that supports parent and child items. See our [project management tool comparison](/project-management-tools/compare/) to shortlist options."),
    ],
